on build_catboost_regressor(), configuring depth=8, iterations=2500, learning_rate=0.08, l2_leaf_reg=5.0, task_type='GPU', and RMSE loss. WHY: Tabular neural networks struggle with spatial coordinate boundaries and produced uncompetitive RMSE (3.8465), while CatBoost's symmetric oblivious trees provide structural architectural diversity alongside LightGBM's leaf-wise and XGBoost's depth-wise hist boosting. HOW: Import CatBoostRegressor from catboost, remove PyTorch TaxiResNet classes and optimizer builders, and implement build_catboost_regressor() with explicit GPU parameter bindings. Preserves standard scikit-learn compatible estimator interfaces across all three models.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Update the execution pipeline to train LightGBM, XGBoost GPU, and CatBoost GPU sequentially with explicit garbage collection, evaluate validation RMSE on the 100k holdout set, optimize ensemble blending weights via Nelder-Mead, clip predictions to the legal minimum of $2.50, and output the submission.csv. WHY: A three-way GBDT ensemble combining leaf-wise, depth-wise, and symmetric trees on 25M training records maximizes variance reduction and model diversity, significantly lowering holdout RMSE. HOW: In main(), sequentially invoke fit() and predict() for LightGBM, XGBoost, and CatBoost, optimize the three blending weights using scipy.optimize.minimize on holdout RMSE, clip test predictions to 2.50, and write exactly 9914 rows to ./submission/submission.csv. Preserves the official RMSE evaluation metric and statutory NYC fare constraints."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
