{"reason": "Execution of the baseline LightGBM model plateaued at a validation RMSE of 3.55635 because orthogonal decision tree splits struggle to partition Manhattan's 29-degree tilted street grid and cannot easily approximate discontinuous, calendar-dependent multiplicative tariff schedules. Historical experiments demonstrate that injecting domain-grounded statutory TLC fare rules, street-aligned rotated coordinates, and river-crossing toll indicators drastically reduces residual variance by transforming the learning objective into modeling traffic delays and bridge tolls. Refocusing feature engineering directly resolves this representational bottleneck while preserving the existing training and evaluation pipeline.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Augment `extract_features` with domain-specific NYC taxi tariff and spatial inductive biases: (1) Compute street-aligned rotated coordinates (`rot_pickup_x`, `rot_pickup_y`, `rot_dropoff_x`, `rot_dropoff_y`) using Manhattan's street grid angle (-28.98 degrees / -0.5058 radians) to enable single orthogonal tree splits to isolate physical street and avenue corridors. (2) Compute an analytical TLC statutory meter fare baseline (`statutory_base_fare`) incorporating the official $2.50 base flag-drop, calendar-dependent mileage rates ($2.00/mile prior to September 4, 2012 vs $2.50/mile after), $0.50 MTA surcharge (post-Nov 1, 2009), $0.50 overnight surcharge (20:00-06:00), $1.00 weekday peak surcharge (16:00-20:00 Mon-Fri), and Manhattan-to-JFK flat rate overrides ($45 pre-hike, $52 post-hike). (3) Engineer river-crossing flags (`cross_east_river`, `cross_hudson_river`) and proximities to major toll crossings (Queens-Midtown Tunnel, Lincoln/Holland Tunnels, RFK Bridge, Verrazzano-Narrows Bridge) to represent detour and toll bottlenecks. Implement these features via vectorized NumPy operations within `extract_features(df: pl.DataFrame, is_test: bool = False)`, maintaining the exact function signature, output dictionary structure, clean data filtering boundaries, and 100k validation holdout split."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "Execution of the baseline LightGBM model plateaued at a validation RMSE of 3.55635 because orthogonal decision tree splits struggle to partition Manhattan's 29-degree tilted street grid and cannot easily approximate discontinuous, calendar-dependent multiplicative tariff schedules. Historical experiments demonstrate that injecting domain-grounded statutory TLC fare rules, street-aligned rotated coordinates, and river-crossing toll indicators drastically reduces residual variance by transforming the learning objective into modeling traffic delays and bridge tolls. Refocusing feature engineering directly resolves this representational bottleneck while preserving the existing training and evaluation pipeline.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Augment `extract_features` with domain-specific NYC taxi tariff and spatial inductive biases: (1) Compute street-aligned rotated coordinates (`rot_pickup_x`, `rot_pickup_y`, `rot_dropoff_x`, `rot_dropoff_y`) using Manhattan's street grid angle (-28.98 degrees / -0.5058 radians) to enable single orthogonal tree splits to isolate physical street and avenue corridors. (2) Compute an analytical TLC statutory meter fare baseline (`statutory_base_fare`) incorporating the official $2.50 base flag-drop, calendar-dependent mileage rates ($2.00/mile prior to September 4, 2012 vs $2.50/mile after), $0.50 MTA surcharge (post-Nov 1, 2009), $0.50 overnight surcharge (20:00-06:00), $1.00 weekday peak surcharge (16:00-20:00 Mon-Fri), and Manhattan-to-JFK flat rate overrides ($45 pre-hike, $52 post-hike). (3) Engineer river-crossing flags (`cross_east_river`, `cross_hudson_river`) and proximities to major toll crossings (Queens-Midtown Tunnel, Lincoln/Holland Tunnels, RFK Bridge, Verrazzano-Narrows Bridge) to represent detour and toll bottlenecks. Implement these features via vectorized NumPy operations within `extract_features(df: pl.DataFrame, is_test: bool = False)`, maintaining the exact function signature, output dictionary structure, clean data filtering boundaries, and 100k validation holdout split."
  }
}"}

